<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Loopback-only cleartext exception for the Google device-link flow.

  ADR #54 2.5 links a device by opening the system browser at Google's consent
  screen and waiting for the redirect back to a listener the app binds on
  127.0.0.1 (kasirmu-bridge/src/desktop_link.rs:54, redirect_uri at :74). That
  redirect is plain http, and Android blocks cleartext by default from API 28+
  (targetSdk is 36 here), so on a RELEASE build Chrome refuses to follow it, the
  callback never arrives, and the wizard times out after its 300s wait with only
  "Could not link this device" to show for it.

  WHY NOT android:usesCleartextTraffic="true": that would permit cleartext to
  EVERY host, including the licence and sync servers, and would let a DNS or
  proxy hijack strip TLS from the app's whole network surface to fix one
  loopback hop. The manifest keeps that flag false for release and this config
  narrows the exception to the literal loopback address.

  127.0.0.1 CANNOT BE INTERCEPTED OFF-DEVICE. The traffic never leaves the
  device or reaches a network interface, so there is no wire to tap and no
  host to spoof - which is why the loopback exemption is the standard one and
  not a hole. `localhost` is deliberately NOT listed: the bridge sends the
  literal 127.0.0.1 because the licence server accepts only that form
  (desktop_link.rs:71-72), and a hosts-file entry cannot re-point 127.0.0.1 the
  way it can re-point a name.

  The debug build still gets android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"
  (build.gradle.kts:52) for the Vite dev server on the LAN, which is a separate
  and development-only need. This file is what makes the flow work in RELEASE.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
